// std.lens_application — sectioned lens-application substrate
// (T-Lens-Application-Surface Slice A; gates #88-#91).
//
// 🟡 TRANSITIONAL. Lands the substrate carriers for applying a lens to
// any `.dag` section (function / module / declaration / expression
// scope). Companion to the file-glob `LensApplication` surface in
// `docs/lens-library-design.md` §3 — that carrier stays for whole-tree
// invariant gates; the carriers below extend `applies_to` from a path
// glob to a structural section reference.
//
// Authority: `docs/design-lens-application-surface.md` (design lock).
// §2 specifies these carriers verbatim; §3 specifies the violation
// routing under fail-closed discipline (`../INVARIANTS.md` C-8).
//
// Why two separate top-level carriers (`EnforcedApplication` +
// `IntrospectApplication`) instead of one sum: v3's `.dag` substrate
// parameterizes sum types uniformly across variants, so a sum where
// Enforce binds Budget but Introspect doesn't is not currently
// expressible. Two top-level carriers sidestep the per-variant-generic
// issue. "SectionedLensApplication" is a collective noun for the pair;
// it is NOT a substrate declaration.
//
// Why the per-lens `LensEnforcement<Output, Budget>` projection rather
// than `Output = Budget` everywhere: complexity's lens-output is rich
// (`ComplexitySummary { work, span, asymptotic_class, ... }`) — required
// for compositional folds — but the user-authored budget is typically a
// single class (`O_log_n`). The projection separates the two: lens
// output stays rich (load-bearing for composition); budget stays simple
// (load-bearing for user authoring). Lenses where Output = Budget (cost,
// parallelism) declare the identity projection.
//
// Per-lens `LensEnforcement` and `EnforceableLens` data declarations
// land alongside each lens (`src/v3/lenses/complexity.dag`, etc.) in
// Slice B; this file only declares the parametric carriers Slice B
// instantiates.
//
// Home: `src/v3/std/` (matches `lens.dag`'s home — references v3-only
// substrate types `DeclarationId` / `NodeId` / `SourceSpan`). Convergence
// trigger mirrors `lens.dag`: when v3 substrate types graduate into
// shared `dsl/std/`, this file moves to `dsl/std/lens_application.dag`.

module v3.std.lens_application

import v3.std.lens { Lens }
import v3.std.substrate { SourceSpan }
import v3.std.substrate_minimal { DeclarationId, NodeId }

// 🟢 TERMINAL. Disjoint sum over the two structural section shapes.
// `DeclarationScope` covers function / module / declaration cases
// uniformly (each lowers to a `DeclarationId`). `NodeScope` is the
// exception for expression-level scope: Node-level expressions live
// inside a Declaration's body sub-DAG and have no DeclarationId of
// their own, so the `NodeId` is qualified by the enclosing
// `DeclarationId` for context.
//
// Why bundle `DeclarationId` into `NodeScope` rather than make it
// "section is just NodeId": declarations are the unit at which lenses
// naturally compose (a complexity lens on a function reads the
// function's body sub-DAG; a complexity lens on an expression reads
// only that expression's sub-tree). Naming the enclosing declaration
// even when narrowing to an expression keeps lens-fold composition
// explicit.
type SectionRef
  = DeclarationScope { declaration: DeclarationId }
  | NodeScope        { declaration: DeclarationId, node: NodeId }

// 🟢 TERMINAL. Single-variant nominal carrier. Per design doc §3 +
// `../INVARIANTS.md` C-8 (fail-closed compilation) + the
// `feedback_fail_closed_discipline` rule, the user's original
// `CompileError | Warning | Silent` framing collapses to "Error only":
//   - Warning is forbidden (C-8 prohibits warnings as steady state).
//   - Silent is forbidden (named exactly in the fail-closed rule as the
//     pattern banned).
// "Introspect mode" replaces both — see `IntrospectApplication` below.
//
// `DiagnosticSeverity` exists as a single-variant carrier (not a bare
// `Unit`) so future *non-fail-closed* surfaces (none currently planned)
// can extend without changing `EnforcedApplication`'s shape. Per
// `feedback_no_metadata_markers`, the named single-variant carrier is
// structurally honest: there is exactly one severity, named.
type DiagnosticSeverity = Error

// 🟢 TERMINAL at the per-lens projection scope. The two fields are the
// irreducible contract a per-lens enforcement must publish:
//
//   - `project`  — extracts the budget-comparable coordinate from the
//                  lens output. Identity for lenses where Output = Budget
//                  (cost, parallelism); for complexity, projects
//                  `ComplexitySummary -> AsymptoticClass`.
//   - `violates` — per-lens violation relation. Returns true iff the
//                  observed (projected) value EXCEEDS the user's
//                  declared budget under the lens's own ordering
//                  (lattice dominance for complexity; cost dominance
//                  for cost; mode-mismatch for parallelism). The
//                  projection alone would leave "did observed exceed
//                  declared?" as API-level convention; the relation
//                  field makes it substrate authority.
type LensEnforcement<Output, Budget> {
  project: fn(Output) -> Budget
  violates: fn(Budget, Budget) -> Bool
}

// 🟢 TERMINAL at the per-lens canonical-pairing scope. Bundles a lens
// with its CANONICAL enforcement (projection + violation relation)
// into ONE substrate authority. `EnforcedApplication` references the
// bundled `EnforceableLens` (not lens + enforcement separately) so the
// application site cannot pair non-canonical lens / enforcement
// combinations.
//
// PARSER-LEVEL UNIQUENESS INVARIANT — TRACKED, NOT YET ENFORCED.
// Per design doc §2, the parser is intended to enforce "at most ONE
// `EnforceableLens<C, B>` declaration per (`Lens<C>`, Budget `B`)
// pair in the program" — same shape as v3's other single-authority
// parser invariants (one declaration per name; one `BoundedLattice`
// instance per type). The check fires at parse time with a Diagnostic
// naming both authoring sites.
//
// **Status at this commit**: the carrier shape is landed (this file);
// the parser check is NOT yet implemented. Until it lands, duplicate
// `EnforceableLens<C, B>` declarations are convention-only — Slice B
// (or a follow-up Substrate slice) lands the parser pass and a
// behavior-driven test that constructs duplicate declarations and
// asserts the Diagnostic. Design lock authority: design doc §2
// "PARSER-LEVEL UNIQUENESS INVARIANT" paragraph.
//
// Why parser-level, not type-level: full type-level enforcement
// (preventing user code from EVER declaring a second
// `EnforceableLens<C, B>`) would require existentials or singleton
// inhabitance — substrate features v3 does not currently express.
// Future substrate hardening lifts this to type-level.
type EnforceableLens<Output, Budget> {
  lens: Lens<Output>
  enforcement: LensEnforcement<Output, Budget>
}

// 🟢 TERMINAL at the enforce-mode application scope. Carrier for
// enforce-mode lens applications. References ONE `EnforceableLens`
// (the bundled lens+enforcement authority); cannot pair against
// arbitrary lens / enforcement combinations.
//
// Per `feedback_state_space_vs_behavioral_invariants` + modeling
// principles 2/6: Enforce-without-budget / Introspect-with-budget /
// Introspect-with-enforcement / lens-projection-budget mismatch are
// all illegal states; all unrepresentable in this carrier shape. The
// shared `Output` and `Budget` type parameters tie
// `enforceable_lens.lens` (`Lens<Output>`),
// `enforceable_lens.enforcement` (`LensEnforcement<Output, Budget>`),
// and `budget` (`Budget`) structurally. Mismatched triples are
// rejected at parse/inference time, not via a separate type-checker
// rule.
//
//   - `enforceable_lens`     — bundled lens+enforcement authority.
//   - `section`              — structural section the application
//                              targets.
//   - `budget`               — user-declared contract; must equal the
//                              `Budget` parameter of `enforceable_lens`.
//   - `diagnostic_severity`  — currently the single-variant `Error`;
//                              extension point for future
//                              non-fail-closed surfaces.
//   - `span`                 — user-authored site for diagnostic
//                              attribution. Required (not Optional) —
//                              every Enforce application has an
//                              authoring site.
type EnforcedApplication<Output, Budget> {
  enforceable_lens: EnforceableLens<Output, Budget>
  section: SectionRef
  budget: Budget
  diagnostic_severity: DiagnosticSeverity
  span: SourceSpan
}

// 🟢 TERMINAL at the introspect-mode application scope. Carrier for
// introspection-mode applications: the lens runs against the section
// and produces a value available to downstream lens composition + debug
// surfaces, but no enforcement and no diagnostic.
//
// Parametric in lens output ONLY — no `Budget`, no enforcement
// projection. Per the design doc P2 / illegal-states-unrepresentable
// concern: an Introspect application MUST NOT carry enforcement
// metadata it cannot consume.
//
// Why a separate top-level carrier rather than a sum variant:
// v3's `.dag` substrate parameterizes sum types uniformly across
// variants — a sum where one variant binds `Budget` and the other
// doesn't is not currently expressible. Two separate top-level
// declarations sidestep this; the lens-fold pass walks the two lists
// separately (per design doc §5).
type IntrospectApplication<Output> {
  lens: Lens<Output>
  section: SectionRef
  span: SourceSpan
}
